WEBVTT

00:00:00.004 --> 00:00:03.272
WHERE controls which source rows stay in the result.

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:06.507
Different questions need different kinds of conditions.

00:00:06.507 --> 00:00:10.942
For direct comparisons, we can use equals, not equal, greater than, greater than or equal to, less than,

00:00:10.942 --> 00:00:13.202
or less than or equal to.

00:00:13.202 --> 00:00:15.067
BETWEEN is convenient for ranges.

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.407
IN is useful when a value may belong to a short list.

00:00:17.407 --> 00:00:20.637
LIKE and PostgreSQL ILIKE support text patterns.

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:23.962
Missing values need IS NULL or IS NOT NULL.

00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:27.312
We can also combine conditions with AND, OR, and NOT.

00:00:27.312 --> 00:00:29.722
A useful habit is to read the condition naturally.

00:00:29.722 --> 00:00:33.927
Age greater than or equal to sixty-five means sixty-five is included.

00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:38.252
When conditions become longer, use parentheses to make the intended grouping obvious rather than depending on

00:00:38.252 --> 00:00:43.082
memory of operator precedence. Think of SELECT work as progressively shaping a question.

00:00:43.082 --> 00:00:48.392
First identify the rows and columns you need, then filter, derive expressions, sort, and limit for inspection.

00:00:48.392 --> 00:00:53.872
A useful habit is to test each part on a small result before adding more complexity,

00:00:53.872 --> 00:00:56.800
especially when a filter contains NULL-sensitive logic.
